{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789789537,"nonce":"H6wKjEetVuGrLMHMd+eFo2bIUWY=","title":"Python: a list, dict or set inside a tuple was stored as its header and copied","message":"A tuple field typed List, Dict or Set was laid out inside the `Type::Tuple`\nvalue struct as the 24-byte growable-list header, by value. Boxing such a\nfield (`zb_list_box_f64` on a tuple's element) boxed the address of the\ncopy inside the tuple, so `(r, v, m) = a` for `a` read out of a typed list\nof tuples printed garbage floats, and nbody's `advance` failed to unpack.\n\nA header has an identity a copy loses (fbbc089), so a tuple stores these\nthree as the box their kind tags (`lower::tuple_field_storage`), and the\nlibrary's `Field::{List, Dict, Set}` compare, print and read them through\nthe box: `ty()` is the box, `boxed` is the identity, `read` is the checked\nunbox re-boxed, eq/lt/repr go through the raw header.\n\nReproducer: a list of `([0.0, 1.0], [3.0, 4.0], 39.4)` tuples, `pairs_of`\nover it, then `for (a, b) in ps: (r1, v1, m1) = a; print(r1, m1)`.","files":null}]}